;; s3-client-vocabulary.wat — PROPOSED names for the CLIENT side of services-as-surfaces
;; (293 S3 `:calls [surface]` + the peer-as-satisfier + the dial). NON-RUNNING; it exists to
;; COMMUNICATE the naming decisions so intueri can weigh which candidates keep their promise.
;;
;; THE ENDPOINT WE ARE NAMING FOR (not the transition): after S4 purges the old per-service
;; internals, EVERY service :satisfies a surface (server) and EVERY consumer :calls a surface
;; (client). Nobody names a concrete service anywhere. The transition names below (marked
;; [TRANSITION — PURGED BY S4]) are scaffolding S2 emitted to dodge a collision; they are NOT
;; candidates — they are the thing being replaced. Name the TRUE endpoint.

;; ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; RATIFIED ANCHORS (already cast + shipped — S1/S2; DO NOT re-name; harmonize WITH these)
;; ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; A surface is the ONE contract; its methods each take a NAMED request record, return a NAMED
;; response record. S1 synthesizes the wire-protocol enums FROM the methods:
(:wat::core::defrecord :probe::Kv::PutRequest  [key <- :wat::core::String  val <- :wat::core::String])
(:wat::core::defrecord :probe::Kv::PutResponse [ok  <- :wat::core::bool])
(:wat::core::defrecord :probe::Kv::GetRequest  [key <- :wat::core::String])
(:wat::core::defrecord :probe::Kv::GetResponse [val <- (:wat::core::Option :wat::core::String)])

(:wat::core::defsurface :probe::Kv :holder :wat::core::Struct
  ;; each method member ALSO registers a METHOD-DISPATCH STUB fn named  :probe::Kv/put ,
  ;; :probe::Kv/get  — receiver typed :probe::Kv (a satisfier), dispatched to the satisfier's
  ;; extend-type impl. THIS is the name the RPC client fn collided with (see below).
  :features [(put [self <- :probe::Kv  req <- :probe::Kv::PutRequest] -> :probe::Kv::PutResponse)
             (get [self <- :probe::Kv  req <- :probe::Kv::GetRequest] -> :probe::Kv::GetResponse)])

;; S1-SYNTHESIZED (shared, one set, under the SURFACE namespace — NOT per service):
(:wat::core::defenum :probe::Kv::Op    :wat::enum::Pure  :Put [req  <- :probe::Kv::PutRequest]  :Get [req  <- :probe::Kv::GetRequest])
(:wat::core::defenum :probe::Kv::Reply :wat::enum::Pure  :Put [resp <- :probe::Kv::PutResponse] :Get [resp <- :probe::Kv::GetResponse])

;; The UNIFORM wire types every satisfier shares (this is the ENTIRE reason a caller dials blind):
;;   (:wat::kernel::Address :- [probe::Kv::Op probe::Kv::Reply])   — the dial target (pure, EDN-crossable)
;;   (:wat::kernel::Peer :- [probe::Kv::Op probe::Kv::Reply])      — what connect yields from that Address
;;   :wat::kernel::connect                                    — the dial: Address -> Peer
;;   :wat::kernel::Locus                                       — the wire (thread/process/UDS/mTLS/L4), orthogonal

;; ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; [TRANSITION — PURGED BY S4] — NOT candidates; the scaffolding being replaced
;; ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; S2, to dodge the :probe::Kv/put dispatch-stub collision, emitted the RPC client fn under the
;; concrete SERVICE namespace: :probe::kv-store'/put  [c <- (Peer :- [Kv::Op Kv::Reply])  req <- ...].
;; That name (a) couples the caller to ONE backend (kv-store'), and (b) can't be named by a
;; :calls [:probe::Kv] consumer that is BLIND to which backend. It dies with the per-service ::Op.

;; ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; THE SLOTS TO NAME (each name is a PROMISE — which candidate keeps it?)
;; ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

;; ── SLOT 1 — the PEER-AS-SATISFIER: the construct that makes a dialed Peer BE a :probe::Kv,
;;    so the SAME surface method dispatches whether the receiver is a local struct or a remote
;;    peer (R31's "the interface and the remote service become one act"). Two shapes:
;;
;;    Shape 1a — NO WRAPPER: the dialed (Peer :- [Kv::Op Kv::Reply]) DIRECTLY satisfies :probe::Kv
;;               (the macro emits an `extend-type :probe::Kv` for the peer type; sending
;;               Kv::Op::Put / receiving Kv::Reply::Put IS the method body). Then there is no
;;               new NAME at all — a Peer of the right protocol is-a Kv. Fewest names, most honest?
;;
;;    Shape 1b — A NAMED CLIENT TYPE that wraps the peer and satisfies the surface. If we need a
;;               name, which promise does it keep? Candidates (a struct holding the Peer, that
;;               extend-types :probe::Kv):
(:wat::core::defstruct :probe::Kv::Client [peer <- :wat::kernel::Peer<probe::Kv::Op,probe::Kv::Reply>])  ;; "a Kv client"
(:wat::core::defstruct :probe::Kv::Remote [peer <- :wat::kernel::Peer<probe::Kv::Op,probe::Kv::Reply>])  ;; "a remote Kv"
(:wat::core::defstruct :probe::Kv::Proxy  [peer <- :wat::kernel::Peer<probe::Kv::Op,probe::Kv::Reply>])  ;; "a stand-in for a Kv"
(:wat::core::defstruct :probe::Kv::Stub   [peer <- :wat::kernel::Peer<probe::Kv::Op,probe::Kv::Reply>])  ;; RPC-gen jargon (CORBA/gRPC)
;;    QUESTION for intueri: does SLOT 1 want a name at all (1a — the Peer is-a Kv, nothing new),
;;    or does a named client type keep a promise the bare peer can't? If named, which of
;;    Client / Remote / Proxy / Stub says what it IS (and does not lie — e.g. Proxy/Stub carry
;;    OOP-RPC-codegen baggage; Remote asserts a locus when a "remote" may be same-process)?

;; ── SLOT 2 — the CLIENT ENTRY POINT the :calls [:probe::Kv] consumer INVOKES to do an op.
;;    If SLOT 1 makes the peer a Kv, then the surface method :probe::Kv/put IS the entry — ONE
;;    name, uniform for local struct AND dialed peer (no separate RPC fn). Is that the true shape,
;;    or is a DISTINCT client-call name wanted? Candidates if distinct:
;;      :probe::Kv/put            — the surface method itself (uniform; requires SLOT-1 peer-is-a-Kv)
;;      :probe::Kv::call/put      — a namespaced RPC face, distinct from the dispatch stub
;;      :probe::Kv/put!           — a bang to mark "this one goes over the wire" (but dispatch may be local...)
;;    QUESTION for intueri: is the honest endpoint ONE name (:probe::Kv/put, dispatched through
;;    whatever satisfies Kv — struct or peer), so the RPC-vs-local distinction is INVISIBLE (the
;;    decomplection R31 promised)? Or does a caller deserve to SEE it may cross the wire?

;; ── SLOT 3 — the DIAL a :calls [:probe::Kv] consumer runs in :init to turn a given
;;    (Address :- [Kv::Op Kv::Reply]) (a pure operating-input) INTO the Kv it then calls.
;;    Today: (:wat::kernel::connect addr) -> Peer. If SLOT 1 = a named client type, the dial
;;    must PRODUCE it. Candidates for the surface-level dial:
;;      (:wat::kernel::connect addr)              — the raw dial (yields Peer; is-a Kv iff SLOT-1a)
;;      (:probe::Kv/connect addr)                  — a surface-level dial that yields a Kv
;;      (:probe::Kv/dial addr)                     — "dial a Kv"
;;      (:probe::Kv/at addr)                       — "the Kv at this address"
;;    QUESTION for intueri: does the consumer just use the ratified `connect` (SLOT-1a: the Peer
;;    is already a Kv, no surface-specific dial needed), or is a surface-level dial name warranted
;;    (and if so, connect / dial / at — which says "produce the Kv I will call" without mumbling)?

;; ── SLOT 4 (bundled, the Reply-as-error-union) — a modeled error is a Reply variant, not just
;;    the success record. The Op enum carries requests; the Reply enum carries SUCCESS (the
;;    response record) PLUS one variant per modeled error (each an error record). What does the
;;    client entry (SLOT 2) RETURN so the caller must branch success-vs-error?
;;      -> :probe::Kv::PutResponse                                  — success only (errors would be lost — dishonest)
;;      -> (:wat::core::Result :- [probe::Kv::PutResponse probe::Kv::Error])   — a Result split
;;      -> :probe::Kv::Reply                                        — the raw Reply enum (caller matches variants)
;;    QUESTION for intueri: what does the client method's RETURN TYPE promise so the caller
;;    cannot ignore a modeled error — the Reply enum itself (match-forced), or a Result over
;;    {success-record, error-union}? (The surface method today declares -> PutResponse; the
;;    Reply-as-error-union changes what the CLIENT sees.)

;; ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; What a GOOD verdict looks like: for each slot, the ONE name (or "no name — use the anchor")
;; that keeps its promise; the rejected candidates marked Level 1 (lies) / Level 2 (mumbles)
;; with why; harmonized with the ratified anchors (Op/Reply/Address/Peer/connect/:satisfies/
;; :impls/:calls) and the endpoint doctrine (nobody names a concrete service; the RPC-vs-local
;; distinction is decomplected away unless a caller genuinely deserves to see the wire).
